 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097691 #edb/ref 1061 3431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20367edf-5f5a-5aec-a12b-3f3cf7c4a01e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097709 #edb/ref 1061 37408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d59ceb2-63e4-5d77-913c-73dbfc0ece9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097810 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10,$ or $100,$ or $1000,$ etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10.$\n\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100.$\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 & |\\times 10 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2 & |\\times 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097810 #edb/ref 1065 1465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097810 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca705a2f-5d99-5642-87c9-9aadf1ef0319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097810 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097811 #edb/ref 1064 "The trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\sin( x + {\\color{red}\\phi})\n$$\n\nis called the **amplitude-phase form** of sine. Here, ${\\color{blue}R}$ is the **amplitude** and ${\\color{red}\\phi}$ is the **phase** of the sine function.\n\nThe sum or difference of a sine function and a cosine function with the same argument can always be written as a sine function in amplitude-phase form. That is to say, if $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\sin x \\pm b \\cos x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\nR \\sin(x \\pm \\phi).\n$$\n\nfor some $R>0$ and $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097811 #edb/ref 1065 2572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097811 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69964ee4-693c-5a43-af92-88232e3d1471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097811 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097812 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been using the amplitude-phase form for sine, but there is also an amplitude-phase form for cosine. If $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\cos x \\pm b \\sin x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\cos(x \\mp {\\color{red}\\phi} )\n$$\n\nfor some ${\\color{blue}R}>0$ and ${\\color{red}\\phi} \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ \n\nNote that the sign in the last formula flips due to the identity $\\cos(x \\mp \\phi) = \\cos x \\cos \\phi \\pm \\sin x \\sin \\phi,$ which reverses the sign when expanding.\n\nWriting this more explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\operatorname{cos}x+b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ a\\operatorname{cos}x-b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's write the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sin{x}+\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form with cosine. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi.$ Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by the second one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(4) \\\\  & \\approx 1.326\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}ϕ+{cos}^{2}ϕ) & =16+1 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =17 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =\\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\operatorname{arctan}(4)) \\\\  & \\approx \\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-1.326)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097812 #edb/ref 1065 2574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097812 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3bb9db1-8b71-5f57-8cd5-5a113632e7d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097812 #edb/ref 1067 "The Amplitude-Phase Form For Cosine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097825 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's write the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi$. Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}ϕ+{sin}^{2}ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x} = {\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{3}} \\sin \\left(x + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}} \\right)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097825 #edb/ref 1065 2887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097825 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81c76ef1-c149-53cf-a9e2-d93a243a7002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097825 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing a Sum or Difference of Sine and Cosine Using Amplitude-Phase Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1064 "It's often helpful to express a given vector in terms of sums and scalar multiples of a handful of known vectors.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following diagram:\n\n![](images/a6/a6c8dcdf9be32345bbc92ff162992e6c9cf02c107de8ff74caf534b508bfe411.png)\n\nHow can we express the vector $\\overrightarrow{PR}$ in terms of the vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ only?\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c72ba23124e9622b50f9daee4006ca4aac6a460b8a694a368d0e53393a85053.png)\n\nFrom the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}}   = \\mathbf{a} \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n{\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QR}}}  = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d284b2d2683ae43fe57e8c96433803bc908d764e489ac0f861ad978393fde7d.png)\n\nTherefore, by substituting these values into our equation for ${\\color{red}\\overrightarrow{PR}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QR} \\\\  & =\\text{a}+(-\\text{b}) \\\\  & =\\text{a}-\\text{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1065 2904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d92705a2-c9e5-5782-91e1-fa086fa5bffb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097840 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations:\n![](images/b6/b6441688c486562e704a29317847ba53ca5130ff6e35c9d4effdea5ede005d3f.svg)\nThe augmented matrix for this system can be written as \n![](images/b4/b45896f12f8195fcb211411a9a2dcd230cee288822e2d37f2b364f6c1c831c6d.svg)\n\nNotice that the matrix is in *row echelon form* (or REF). Whenever the augmented matrix is in row echelon form, we can solve the system using a method called **back substitution**.\n\nBack substitution involves\n\n- starting with the lowest equation (which is already solved for one variable),\n\n- substituting the result into the next-lowest equation to find the solution for another variable, and\n\n- continuing to substitute the result into the next-lowest equation until the values of all the variables have been found.\n\nHere, the third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny -(3) = 2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $y=5$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx + 2(5) + (3) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = -12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3.$ We can write it in vector form, as follows:\n\n![](images/8b/8bbbabd0167fc73c58c095fb11ed5efb2c2dca63ee90e95717e26f30f0f170e9.svg)\n\n**Note:** The values $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3$ together form *one* solution. We say that this system has *one* solution, *not* three solutions. A system with three solutions would have three different solution vectors, whereas here, we only have *one* solution vector." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097840 #edb/ref 1065 3149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097840 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d84a9a0-94f9-57e2-a8e6-333ba26d72e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097840 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097841 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/67/673ea82819858b9a46e0e351a8d6f41b069ed039ee0d31a382110f938f7ef72a.svg)\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n![](images/35/35627a034b30736abb057c01f3251fc047dce780a8cbcf389f8b5c2c2317d766.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/32/32a252e05e299a206f2f3776a2a04419f21726e1f8f542cfd4f328dac3251f21.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097841 #edb/ref 1065 3155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097841 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d8cb1c1-9e09-560f-83ad-7c8a3d0a78cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097841 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097842 #edb/ref 1064 "The **standard Gaussian elimination** for a $3 \\times 3$ system can be described as follows:\n\n- If $a_{11} \\neq 0$ and $a_{11} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1/a_{11}.$\n\n- We use first row and the necessary row operations to make the remaining entries of the first column below $a_{11}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{21} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{21})R_1;$\n\n- if $a_{31} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{31})R_1.$\n\n- If $a_{22} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2/a_{22}.$\n\n- We use the second row and the necessary row operation to make the entry below $a_{22}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{32} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{32})R_2.$\n\n- If $a_{33} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3/a_{33}.$\n\n- In some exceptional cases, such as when the element on the diagonal is equal to $0,$ we first need to exchange rows. Then proceed using the same steps as above.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097842 #edb/ref 1065 3156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097842 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "955c2b9a-3d5f-52c3-b4f3-62541732ffcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097842 #edb/ref 1067 "General Algorithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7c/7c2283c7de572acfc58b443499598554ecf3d26b1a13c48c80b8d6aadf7b576a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form, but we can transform it even further into the **reduced row echelon form** (RREF) using the standard **Gauss-Jordan** method. \n\nThe algorithm is similar to the algorithm of converting a matrix to row echelon form, except that we need to make *all* the pivot entries equal to $1$ and *all* the entries below *and above* a pivot (in a pivot column) equal to $0.$\n\nIn our case, we convert $M$ to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final matrix is in reduced row echelon form:\n\n- all the pivot entries are equal to $1$ (here we have $\\color{red}1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- all the entries below and above a pivot are equal to ${\\color{blue}0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1065 3160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce056e74-9707-52d3-b172-483ca14e1f8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097843 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1064 "For a $3\\times 3$ system with $3$ pivots, the Gauss-Jordan method can be described as follows:\n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n![](images/5c/5c0f5ac3d95843b142c90e8ce83378ea2b2ad693baba6670e0cde46a449f5225.svg)\n\n- We use the third row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{33}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{23} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{23})R_3$, and\n\n- if $a_{13} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{13})R_3$.\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/9c/9c817628a3c646a928f12548a109b8136bb0a5977fab6e4d18e972690d81159b.svg)\n\n**Note:** Unlike the row echelon form, the *reduced* row echelon form of a matrix is unique." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1065 3161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f74e6075-9c2a-5495-ab21-470711559f2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097844 #edb/ref 1067 "General Algorithm (RREF)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1064 "When a $3\\times3$ system of equations has two pivot columns instead of three, we can still reduce its augmented matrix to reduced row echelon form. The process is similar to what we did before. \n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n ![](images/99/992cfaabdb3490e99553fbbdfee0089b399306e3e65720fc6a70014bfa35321a.svg)\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/3d/3da5751a339ff2ee85d424b42a72f5270f1be076407a33abd1b79e0f082639d5.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1065 3949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "baed23fb-560e-56ad-aea3-46f3728ebfff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097869 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With Two Pivots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1064 "It's also possible to write the augmented matrix for a $3\\times 3$ system of linear equations with only one pivot in reduced row echelon form. \n\nLet's see an example of how this works." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1065 3950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3748ee3-9d5d-5a6a-808f-ef8f99cbd1aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097870 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With One Pivot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 & |\\,\\times 4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1065 8706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68821d79-90e2-580a-95fd-a5268f848ff4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097919 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also use the substitution method to solve systems of equations containing fractional coefficients.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we multiply both equations by the lowest common denominator of the fractions in the system.\n\n- Then, we apply the substitution method as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1065 9138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33f3d90d-d232-500a-b9c6-cecc2546b571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097930 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100891 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100892 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100954 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100954 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100954 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262891 #edb/r