){(4)}^{4}{(-x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(-x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(-x)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(-x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(-x)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =256-256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101712 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101712 #edb/ref 1061 18912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101712 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41da2623-7af5-5f79-be76-db13c7501f64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101712 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101713 #edb/ref 1061 18913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ce725e9-9f59-5a2d-9d61-fd95ca45d571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101714 #edb/ref 1061 18914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "453c1e8e-a1de-5e5a-88c3-7f39f95701f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101801 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101801 #edb/ref 1061 36724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101801 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5075d69a-6b72-5201-872b-77da45eaf068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101801 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101837 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101837 #edb/ref 1061 424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101837 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1483e1b-2de0-5e5b-a8d1-f4d81897891d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101837 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101838 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101838 #edb/ref 1061 425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101838 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51e2792d-7ab3-57f6-a8aa-4475d65fedf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101838 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101839 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101839 #edb/ref 1061 428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101839 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1dad1df-a9ec-5939-84eb-1443d3e7f165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1061 430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2bafd4a-88b6-500a-b45d-6de37cdf6804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1061 431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a5952ff-0a94-5306-8005-3e5b53635c33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101841 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101842 #edb/ref 1061 433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101842 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcca1c4b-c754-54c1-a87f-df2f0b306855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101842 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3}+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1065 236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "80db4900-d9c5-5900-b975-5c26b8f713e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101931 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1065 239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "416b850c-1c8d-5794-a787-3a59d48d0616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101932 #edb/ref 1067 "Limits of Piecewise Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101976 #edb/ref 1064 "The **binomial theorem** states that for any real numbers $a$ and $b$ and any positive integer $n,$ \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n},\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that \n\n$$\n{n \\choose k} = \\dfrac{n!}{k!\\, (n-k)!  }.\n$$\n\nLet's use the binomial theorem to find the first few terms in the expansion of\n\n$$\n(2+x)^6.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a = 2,$ $b= x,$ and $n=6.$  Therefore, the first three terms of our expansion are given by\n\n$$\n{\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{6}}   =  \n{6\\choose0}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6}}{\\left( x \\right)^0} +  {6\\choose1}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6 - 1}}{\\left( x \\right)^1} +  {6\\choose2}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6 - 2}}{\\left( x \\right)^2} + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n{6\\choose0} = {\\color{blue}{1}}, \\qquad {6\\choose1} = {\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad {6\\choose2} = {\\color{blue}{15}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expansion can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0}){(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1}){(2)}^{6-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2}){(2)}^{6-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =(1)({2}^{6})(1)+(6)({2}^{5})(x)+(15)({2}^{4})({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =(1)(64)(1)+(6)(32)(x)+(15)(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =64+192\\,x+240\\,{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101976 #edb/ref 1065 3388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101976 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "426cf476-6bfd-5054-aac9-8200164a34b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101976 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105745 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105745 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105745 #edb/ref 1038 #ed